  "query_preview": "..Very strong high pressure remains settled across \nArizona today, with little movement expected over the next couple of \ndays. This will allow for a slight drying trend, though moisture \ndoesn't look to fully abate across the region. Showers and \nthunderstorms will remain isolated each day, remaining focused \nacross the higher terrain and mainly towards the White Mountains \neach day. Storm activity will remain diurnally driven with showers \nand storms dwindling in coverage as we head into the evening each \nday. \n\nWith the downturn in storm activity, we will see increasing heat \nbuild across the region. Expect hot daytime temperatures to \ncontinue, likely the hottest of the season so far. Extreme Heat \nWarnings and Heat Advisories remain in place through early next \nweek. Lower elevations of the Grand Canyon may see the heat continue \ninto Wednesday but with some uncertainty as to the placement of the \nhigh, we have held off on any additional extensions. \n\nThe high starts to elongate on Sunday and is strong enough to \nsuppress any approaching troughs through the week. Moisture looks to \nincrease across the region  for the first half of the week, with \nchances for scattered showers and storms each day, especially across \nthe higher terrain. As we head towards the latter half of next week, \nthe high will start to shift. Models are split on whether the shift \nwill be to the north or the west and each will have different \nimpacts for the region. A more westerly shift would mean drier and \nhotter conditions, while a northerly shift would signal increasing \nmoisture, cooler temperatures, and potential for thunderstorms each \nday. The uncertainty with the timing and placement of the high means \nthere is less confidence in the forecast, especially for Thursday \nonward. However, the ensemble guidance has been trending towards \nmore active monsoon as we move through the week. Stay tuned!",
